<Base
  annotatable
  title="Jason Cusati — A completeness benchmark for agentic research tooling"
  description="A planned study measuring how much of the research and paper-writing lifecycle the agentic-research toolkit covers, and whether its checks catch what they claim to."
>
  <p class="crumbs"><a href={project}>← Governed Agentic Empirical Research</a></p>
  <h1>Does the tooling cover the work?</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">A completeness benchmark for agentic-research · planned study, no results yet</p>
  <p class="lede">
    <a href={`${project}agentic-research/`}>agentic-research</a> grew one fix at a time, each prompted by
    something that went wrong on a real paper. That is a good way to build a tool and a poor way to know
    what it misses. This study asks the question directly: set against what doing research and writing a
    paper actually involves, what does the tool support, what does it only gesture at, and what does it leave
    entirely to the researcher?
  </p>

  <h2>Why existing benchmarks don't answer this</h2>
  <p>
    Benchmarks for research agents mostly score the output of an autonomous run.
    <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.01848">PaperBench</a> has agents replicate ICML papers from scratch,
    graded against rubrics written with the original authors.
    <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.07591">ResearchClawBench</a> hides a published paper and asks agents to
    re-discover it from its literature and raw data.
    <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.12788">ARAC-Bench</a> comes closest in spirit. It scores how completely
    an autonomous system's trajectory covers expected methodology across proposal, experiment and synthesis,
    and finds the experiment stage is where systems fall down.
    <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.07462">AARRI-Bench</a> runs tasks across several agent harnesses and
    finds that the model matters far more than the harness. On the writing side,
    <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.01128">paper-reconstruction evaluation</a> checks AI-written papers
    for presentation quality and hallucination, and
    <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.28631">a multi-model review study</a> asks whether AI reviewers can
    rank AI-scientist systems at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    All of these treat the agent as the researcher. agentic-research is built on the opposite assumption: a
    human does the research, and the tool scaffolds, checks and gates the work around them. For a tool like
    that, the useful questions are different. Does it cover the lifecycle? Do its gates actually close? Where
    does it hand a decision back to the human, and does it say so?
  </p>

  <h2>Research questions</h2>
  <ol class="rqs">
    <li><strong>Coverage.</strong> Which activities of the research and paper-writing lifecycle does the tool support, and how strongly?</li>
    <li><strong>Enforcement.</strong> Do its mechanical checks catch the defects they claim to catch, including slightly disguised ones?</li>
    <li><strong>The human role.</strong> At which activities does the tool hand judgment to a person, and is that hand-off explicit in the tool or left implicit?</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>How it will work</h2>
  <h3>Stage 1: a coverage audit</h3>
  <p>
    The reference is a lifecycle model built from published sources, with every activity traced back to the
    item it came from: the general standard of the
    <a href="https://www2.sigsoft.org/EmpiricalStandards/">ACM SIGSOFT Empirical Standards</a>, the 14
    contributor roles of <a href="https://niso.org/publications/z39104-2022-credit">CRediT (ANSI/NISO
    Z39.104-2022)</a>, the four research stages of the
    <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.04306">LLM4SR survey</a>, the six-stage workflow of a
    <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23045">recent survey of AI scientists</a>, and ARAC-Bench's stage rubric.
    A script inventories every skill, agent, command, check and template in a pinned version of the plugin.
    Two people then code each activity independently on a four-step scale:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Enforced</td><td>A mechanical check fails when the activity isn't done.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Procedural</td><td>An agent or skill procedure covers it, but nothing fails if it is skipped.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Scaffolded</td><td>A template or folder exists for it, with no procedure behind it.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Absent</td><td>Nothing in the tool addresses it.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Agreement between the coders is reported before they reconcile. An agent's proposed coding can be compared against theirs, but it is never used as the answer key.</p>

  <h3>Stage 2: do the gates close?</h3>
  <p>
    For every claim the plugin makes about what its checker catches, a script injects that defect into a
    clean fixture paper and records whether the checker fails. Each fault also gets disguised variants, such
    as a citation command with options or a placeholder inside a comment. Clean fixtures run alongside as
    controls that must pass. No model is involved, so every run is deterministic and repeatable from one
    command. Runs where an agent performs the plugin's procedures, for example verifying a reference list
    that has fabricated entries mixed in, are designed and pre-registered now. They will run only once
    thresholds and a budget are set.
  </p>

  <h3>The tool is also a subject</h3>
  <p>
    The study's own repository is being set up by OpenCode, a different agent harness, following the
    plugin's procedures as written for Claude Code. A step-by-step log of what it could follow, what it had
    to adapt and what it could not do is a direct test of the harness-independence gap.
  </p>

  <h2>Where people decide</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The lifecycle reference model is approved by a person before anything is scored against it.</li>
    <li>Coverage coding is done by two people. An agent's version is a comparison, never the gold standard.</li>
    <li>Pass thresholds and the budget for agent runs are set by a person and committed before those runs happen.</li>
    <li>Every change to the study repository goes through governance: draft PRs, checks, human merge.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Status</h2>
  <p>
    Planned. The design and tooling are being built in a private repository as of 9 November 2026, and a
    workshop paper is in preparation. No result on this page should be read as a finding until it appears
    here with its data.
  </p>
</Base>

